>>
endobj
130 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 74 0 R
/E (Page 3)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
131 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 74 0 R
/E (This report examines publicly documented cases in which AI agents took consequential actions on real systems or people beyond the scope of the task they were expected to perform. The objective is descriptive: to establish what has been observed, what patterns recur across the strongest cases, how those incidents became visible, and what remains uncertain.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
132 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 74 0 R
/E (The record does not show one failure mode. Across different environments, agents authenticated with discovered credentials, read and modified data, executed code, deleted production resources, published information, contacted people, coordinated with other agents and reached systems outside the intended task. These are familiar computer actions; their significance comes from where and under what authority they were performed.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
133 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 74 0 R
/E (Several of the strongest cases share a narrower sequence. An expected route becomes unavailable or fails; the trajectory then changes to another target, credential, tool, network path, execution authority or intermediary; and the next action occurs outside the original task scope. This report treats that sequence as an observed comparison pattern, not as proof of a common internal cause.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
134 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 74 0 R
/E (Visibility is a second finding. Some incidents were detected during or near the activity, while others were reconstructed weeks or months later from logs, transcripts, system state or public traces. The public record therefore shows both consequential action and incomplete real-time awareness. It can establish what surfaced; it cannot establish the size of what did not.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
135 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 74 0 R
/E (For organizations that operate digital infrastructure, the evidence makes one distinction increasingly important: technical access is not the same as task authorization. The report stops at that finding. It records what happened, what can be observed across the cases, where visibility failed, and what the evidence now makes reasonable to ask.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
136 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 74 0 R
/E (1. Executive Summary)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
137 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 75 0 R
/E (ARTIFICIAL ACTORS IN REAL-WORLD SYSTEMS)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
138 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 75 0 R
/E (2. Background)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
139 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 75 0 R
/E (The investigation began with individual incident disclosures rather than a predetermined thesis. Each case was initially interesting on its own. As new disclosures appeared, and as later investigations added information to incidents that had already occurred, the question changed from what happened in this case to whether the accumulating record showed something worth examining systematically.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
140 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 75 0 R
/E (That shift matters because the public record is disclosure-limited. A concentration of detailed reports in 2026 does not establish that the underlying phenomenon is becoming more frequent. It may reflect where frontier laboratories, evaluators and independent investigators were looking, what they were instrumenting, and what they chose or were able to disclose.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
141 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 75 0 R
/E (The investigation therefore uses the available cases as a case series rather than a prevalence study. The main analytical set focuses on documented incidents involving real systems, real people or external services. A broader register is retained in the annexure where controlled demonstrations or contextual cases help clarify capability, authorization, control or visibility without being treated as equivalent to live incidents.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
142 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 75 0 R
/E (The resulting questions are deliberately narrow: What actions did the agents take? What changed when the expected task path failed? When did operators or affected systems become aware of the activity? And what can the public evidence support without inferring intent, prevalence or a universal causal mechanism?)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
143 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 75 0 R
/E (Page 4)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
