/E (The evidence suggests two distinct visibility problems. The organization running or evaluating the )
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
278 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 80 0 R
/E (agent may fail to recognize a scope change while it is happening. Separately, the receiving system or affected organization may not recognize that an artificial actor is interacting with it, why it is there, or whether the action is authorized for the task.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
279 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 80 0 R
/E (The DNS incident provides one of the clearest published timing sequences: approximately 12 minutes from the first external response to the P0 alert, about 3 additional minutes to human acknowledgement, and approximately 2 hours 44 minutes from the external response to termination of the run. The case demonstrates that detection, human recognition and intervention are different events.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
280 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 80 0 R
/E (At the other end of the spectrum, Anthropic's January cyber-evaluation incident and OpenAI's Australian-government activity were understood later through retrospective review. Independent reconstruction of Australian activity further illustrates that public traces can add visibility after the fact without revealing the full unseen population of events.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
