305 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 82 0 R
/E (ARTIFICIAL ACTORS IN REAL-WORLD SYSTEMS)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
306 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 82 0 R
/E (Page 11)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
307 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 82 0 R
/E (The purpose of this first investigation is deliberately limited: to look closely at what is happening. The public record now contains enough documented evidence to show that artificial actors can move beyond the scope of an intended task and produce real effects in human systems. It does not tell us how often this happens, how much remains unseen, or why every trajectory unfolds as it does.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
308 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 82 0 R
/E (What concerns me most after reviewing the record is visibility. A receiving system may not know that an artificial actor is interacting with it, why it is there, what task it was given, or what authority it is supposed to have. Even when the actor begins with legitimate access, the more difficult question is whether the system can recognize the moment its trajectory changes, particularly when that change can occur at machine speed.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
309 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 82 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
310 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 82 0 R
/E (That shifts part of the problem from controlling the model to considering the design of the environment it encounters. Technical access should not automatically become task permission. Sensitive routes, high-consequence actions and changes to real systems raise questions about what controls exist at the point where capability becomes action, particularly when those controls cannot depend on the actor correctly describing its own purpose or choosing to stop.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
311 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 82 0 R
/E (There is also an asymmetry worth examining. Frontier laboratories have the infrastructure and experience to observe emerging capability first; the businesses and institutions with which those models may interact do not necessarily have the same visibility. This raises a broader question for AI security and cybersecurity: what defensive capabilities will receiving systems need as artificial actors become more capable? The next questions may concern how systems recognize artificial actors, detect changes in task scope or behaviour as they occur, and test whether existing controls remain effective against capable agents. These are not proposed solutions, but directions for further investigation into how defensive capability may need to evolve alongside agent capability.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
312 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 82 0 R
/E (This report does not resolve those questions, and the evidence does not support prescribing a single defensive architecture. It establishes the conditions that make the questions worth asking. We may not be able to predict every route an artificial actor will take. We can decide what our systems will permit when it gets there.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
313 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 82 0 R
/E (8. Final Remarks)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [8]
>>
